Title: Graph Traversal
You are designing a navigation system for a campus with multiple buildings. The system
should explore possible paths (routes) using BFS or DFS.
Create a graph using an adjacency matrix and implement Breadth-First Search and
Depth-First Search to explore the building connectivity.
Software Used: ‘C’ programming tool (DEV C++)
Theory:
What is a Graph?
A Graph is a non-linear data structure consisting of vertices and edges. The vertices are
sometimes also referred to as nodes and the edges are lines or arcs that connect any two
nodes in the graph. More formally a Graph is composed of a set of vertices( V ) and a set of
edges( E ). The graph is denoted by G (E, V).
A graph is a data structure consisting of:
 A set of vertices (nodes) – in this case, buildings.
 A set of edges (connections) – representing direct paths between buildings
Graphs can be:
a. Undirected (bidirectional paths)
b. Directed (one-way paths)

They can be represented in two common ways:
1. Adjacency Matrix – 2D array of size V x V
2. Adjacency List – array of linked lists

Real Time Exmple: Campus as a Graph
 Each building on the campus is a node.
 A path (like a road or hallway) between two buildings is an edge.
 The goal is to explore connectivity (reachable buildings) using graph traversal algorithms.



Graph Traversal
1. Breadth-First Search (BFS)
 Explores the graph level by level.
 Uses a queue.
 Best for finding the shortest path in unweighted graphs.

Steps:
1. Start from a source node.
2. Visit all neighbors.
3. Then visit their neighbors.
4. Continue until all reachable nodes are visited.
2. Depth-First Search (DFS)

 Explores as deep as possible before backtracking.
 Uses recursion or a stack.

Steps:
1. Start at a source node.
2. Visit one neighbor, then that neighbor’s neighbor, and so on.
3. Backtrack when no more neighbors to visit.

Advantages of Graph :
1. By using graphs we can easily find the shortest path, neighbors of the nodes, and many more.
2. Graphs are used to implement algorithms like DFS and BFS.
3. It is used to find minimum spanning tree which has many practical applications.
4. It helps in organizing data.
5. Because of its non-linear structure, helps in understanding complex problems and their
visualization
Disadvantages of Graph:
1. Graphs use lots of pointers which can be complex to handle.
2. It can have large memory complexity.



3. If the graph is represented with an adjacency matrix then it does not allow parallel edges and
multiplication of the graph is also difficult.
Algorithm:
1. BFS (Breadth-First Search)
BFS(graph, start):
Create a visited[ ] array and initialize to false
Create a queue and enqueue start node
Mark start as visited
While queue is not empty:
Dequeue node and process it
For all neighbors of the node:
If not visited:
Mark visited and enqueue
2. DFS (Depth-First Search)
DFS(graph, node, visited[]):
Mark node as visited
Process the node
For all neighbors of node:
If not visited:
Call DFS recursively
